




<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Write each quadratic function in the standard form <m>ax^2+bx+c</m> and identify <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>. For each function, what is the <m>y</m>-intercept? Will its graph open up or down?
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=(-4x)^2+1</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Because <m>(-4x)^2 = 16x^2</m>, then the equation in standard form is
<md>
y = 16x^2 + 1.
</md> This means <m>a=16</m>, <m>b=0</m>, and <m>c=1</m>. Because <m>a</m> is positive, the quadratic opens up. And the vertical intercept is <m>1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>f(x)=(x-6)^2-20</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First, expand <m>(x-6)^2</m> by multiplying <m>(x-6)(x-6)</m> to get
<md>
f(x) = (x-6)(x-6)-20 = (x^2-6x-6x+36)-20 = x^2-12x+16.
</md> This means <m>a=1</m>, <m>b=-12</m>, and <m>c=16</m>. Because <m>a</m> is positive, the graph opens up. And the vertical intercept is <m>16</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>g(x)=2(x-3)^2-16</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Expand the <m>(x-3)^2</m> term by computing <m>(x-3)(x-3)</m>:
</p>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>g(x) \amp= 2(x-3)(x-3)-16</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2 \pbr{x^2-3x-3x+9} -16</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 2\pbr{x^2-6x+9} -16</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2x^2-12x+18-16</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2x^2-12x + 2</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
So <m>a=2</m>, <m>b=-12</m>, and <m>c=2</m>. This means the graph opens up and has a vertical intercept of <m>2</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=-(x+1)(x-5)</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Expand the product to get
<md>
y = - \pbr{x^2-5x+x-5} = - \pbr{x^2-4x-5}  = -x^2+4x+5.
</md> Be careful of the negative sign in front! Now, <m>a=-1</m>, <m>b=4</m>, and <m>c=5</m> so the graph opens down (because <m>a</m> is negative) and has a vertical intercept of <m>5</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>h(x)=(x-2)(x+7)-3(2x-5)</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>

<p>
<md>
<mrow>h(x) \amp= x^2+7x-2x-14 - 3(2x-5)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= x^2+5x-14 - 6x + 15</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= x^2-x+1</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Therefore <m>a=1</m>, <m>b=-1</m>, and <m>c=1</m>, so the graph opens up and has a vertical intercept at <m>1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



</exercise>
 

<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
A quadratic parabola crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at the point <m>(2,0)</m> and has a vertex of <m>(4,4)</m>. Find the second point on the <m>x</m>-axis where the parabola crosses the <m>x</m>-axis. Does the parabola open up or open down?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Recall that <m>h=\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2} </m>. We know <m>x_1=2</m> and <m>h=4</m> (the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the vertex). Therefore
<md>
4=\frac{2+x_2}{2} .
</md> Solving for <m>x_2</m>, we multiply both sides by <m>2</m> to get <m>8 = 2+x_2</m> and so <m>x_2=6</m>. This means that the function has another <m>x</m>-intercept at <m>x=6</m>.

To determine if it opens up or down, note that the vertex is above the <m>x</m>-axis (because <m>k=4</m>, the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the vertex). So the graph must open <em>down</em>, because we know it has two horizontal intercepts and the vertex is above the <m>x</m>-axis.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
The horizontal intercepts of a quadratic parabola are <m>x=-2</m> and <m>x=5</m>. Find the <m>x</m>-coordinate of its vertex.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The <m>x</m>-coordinate of the vertex is <m>h</m>, and so we use
<md>
h = \frac{x_1+x_2}{2} .
</md> Because <m>x_1=-2</m> and <m>x_2=5</m>, then
<md>
h = \frac{-2+5}{2}  = \frac{3}{2}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>





